---
layout: post
title: "Week 2: Developing an Audio Tour Guide"
date: 2026-06-18
---

On the second week, we worked on developing an Audio Guide by adapting source code from the Australian Center for Moving the Moving Image (ACMI). 

Using the existing repository by ACMI, resources from Mathaf, and ElevenLabs text to speech function, I created an audioguide for the Mathaf 15th anniversary exhibition, *Resolutions*. 

During the process, I was able to learn more about the Resolutions exhibition at Mathaf. Although I had already taken a tour of the museum and gotten the gist of the exhibition, this project helped me better understand it as I read more about the exhibition to create the tour. In addition, I enjoyed editing the layout of the website and using the Mathaf colours to customize the page. 

This project also allowed me to explore AI text to speech for the first time using the ElevenLabs voice generator. 

The most challenging aspect of this project for me was the fact that I was working with code that I was unfamiliar with. It took time for me to get used to the structure of the repository 
and learn where each aspect is stored in order to edit it. In general, the README.md file of the repository helped, but there was still a learning curve. I attempted to open the repository to run it from my local device so that I could see the changes I created faster than committing directly to the branch. However, I got some errors that it couldn’t be run due to the repository using older versions of the software. It was still able to run when I edited the files directly in github, but it took longer to see each change as I had to wait for the changes to be implemented. 

Although it took some time to learn the structure of the repository, using an existing base still made it faster to develop the audio guide than if it had been made from scratch. 

If I were to spend more time on this project, I would work on further customizing the page to make it suit Mathaf’s identity. The header at the top simply states “Audio Guide,” which I feel is quite generic. However, I found that it is actually an image within the code and not simply writing, which makes it a little more challenging to change. In addition, the navigation buttons are also icons, which means that the colours of the back button, menu, etc, cannot be changed unless the image is edited. 

Overall this project was insightful and gave me valuable experience in working with existing codebases. It also strengthened my confidence in navigating unfamiliar technical environments and reinforced the importance of understanding both the structure and logic behind a project in order to effectively customize and extend it.

Find the project here: https://jillian-nerice.github.io/static-museum-audio-guide/welcome/ 


